<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The privacy manifest (plan 4.3). Required since May 2024. It is bundled
  into the app as a resource by ios/project.yml.

  Three claims, all of them true of this app as the Android app is true of it:

    * NSPrivacyTracking false and no tracking domains. There is no analytics
      SDK, no advertising SDK and no crash-reporting SDK in this app, and the
      licences test fails the build if one ever appears.
    * NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes empty. Everything the app sends goes to the
      household's own server, which Coders Farm never sees; problem reports go
      by the person's own email, from their own mail app. Apple's definition
      of "collected" is data leaving the device in a way that lets US or a
      partner of ours get at it, which is nothing here.
    * The required-reason APIs below: ONLY the categories the code calls,
      each with a reason code from Apple's published list.

  Re-audited on 2026-11-14 by grepping ios/Domovoi and ios/Packages for every
  API in Apple's five categories. Two categories that were declared before
  are not used by any code and came out (declaring an API we do not call is
  a claim we should not make):

    * Disk space (was E174.1). Nothing calls volumeAvailableCapacity*,
      systemFreeSize, statfs or statvfs. The model download it was meant for
      is not in the iPhone app. Put E174.1 back in the batch that adds a
      free-space check before a download.
    * System boot time (was 35F9.1). Nothing calls systemUptime or
      mach_absolute_time. Monotonic time is clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)
      in TokenHealth.swift, ContinuousClock and DispatchTime, none of which
      is on Apple's list.
    * Active keyboards: never used (no activeInputModes).

  When any of that stops being true (a phone lookup to Wikipedia, an HTTPS
  report destination of ours, any service of ours, a new required-reason
  call) this file and the App Store privacy label change in the same batch,
  and the privacy policy the same day, as for Play. domovoi/tests/
  test_ios_store.py checks this file against the code.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		  UserDefaults, always UserDefaults.standard: the app's own
		  preferences (DeviceRegistry.UserDefaultsStore, AlertsStore,
		  UserDefaultsSavedState for chat pins, heads-ups, the last chat place,
		  the picked-folder bookmarks). CA92.1: "to read and write information
		  that is only accessible to the app itself". The app is in no app
		  group and shares nothing (no suiteName anywhere), so 1C8F.1 does not
		  apply.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  File timestamps. The one call is PhoneFolderSource.page in
		  Packages/DomovoiKit/Sources/DomovoiKit/PhoneMediaSource.swift: it
		  reads .contentModificationDateKey for the files in a folder the
		  person picked with the document picker (a security-scoped bookmark,
		  PhoneFolderBookmarks.swift). 3B52.1: "to access the timestamps,
		  size, or other metadata of files or directories that the user
		  specifically granted access to, such as using a document picker view
		  controller".

		  Not C617.1: nothing reads a timestamp of a file in the app's own
		  container. PHAsset.creationDate (PhonePhotosSource.swift) is Photos
		  metadata, not a file-timestamp API. Add C617.1 beside this one if a
		  timestamp of the app's own files is ever read.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>3B52.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
